# Mobile Navigation Patterns

Expo Router uses `react-navigation` under the hood. Choose the right primitive:

## Stack — for forward/back drill-down

```tsx
import { Stack } from 'expo-router';

<Stack
  screenOptions={{
    headerStyle: { backgroundColor: Colors[scheme].tint },
    headerTintColor: '#fff',
    animation: 'slide_from_right',
  }}
>
  <Stack.Screen name="index" options={{ title: 'Home' }} />
  <Stack.Screen name="settings" options={{ title: 'Settings' }} />
</Stack>
```

Use when: linear flow, back button makes sense, screens have semantic hierarchy.

## Tabs — for top-level sections

```tsx
import { Tabs } from 'expo-router';
import { IconSymbol } from '@/components/ui/icon-symbol';
import { HapticTab } from '@/components/haptic-tab';

<Tabs
  screenOptions={{
    tabBarActiveTintColor: Colors[scheme].tint,
    tabBarButton: HapticTab,
    headerShown: false,
  }}
>
  <Tabs.Screen
    name="index"
    options={{
      title: 'Home',
      tabBarIcon: ({ color }) => <IconSymbol size={28} name="house.fill" color={color} />,
    }}
  />
</Tabs>
```

Use when: 2-5 peer sections users switch between freely. Never put a tab bar inside another tab bar.

## Modal — for transient flows

```tsx
// app/_layout.tsx
<Stack.Screen
  name="modal"
  options={{
    presentation: 'modal',
    gestureEnabled: true,
    headerShown: true,
    title: 'Create item',
  }}
/>

// app/modal.tsx
import { router } from 'expo-router';

export default function Modal() {
  return (
    <View>
      <Form onSubmit={(values) => { /* ... */; router.back(); }} />
    </View>
  );
}
```

Use when: short user flow, dismiss via swipe/back, no navigation history within.

## Drawer — for occasional side navigation

```tsx
import { Drawer } from 'expo-router/drawer';

<Drawer>
  <Drawer.Screen name="(tabs)" options={{ drawerLabel: 'Home' }} />
  <Drawer.Screen name="settings" options={{ drawerLabel: 'Settings' }} />
</Drawer>
```

Use sparingly — drawers are an iOS anti-pattern for primary navigation. Prefer tabs or stack.

## Haptics on interactions

Use `expo-haptics` for tab presses and key actions:

```tsx
// src/components/haptic-tab.tsx
import * as Haptics from 'expo-haptics';
import { Pressable } from 'react-native';

export function HapticTab({ children, ...props }: PressableProps) {
  return (
    <Pressable
      {...props}
      onPressIn={() => Haptics.impactAsync(Haptics.ImpactFeedbackStyle.Light)}
    >
      {children}
    </Pressable>
  );
}
```

## Safe areas

Always respect safe areas on screens:

```tsx
import { useSafeAreaInsets } from 'react-native-safe-area-context';

const insets = useSafeAreaInsets();
<View style={{ paddingTop: insets.top, paddingBottom: insets.bottom }} />
```

The root layout wraps everything in `<SafeAreaProvider>`. Don't re-wrap children.

## Gestures

Use `react-native-gesture-handler` primitives for swipes, drags, pinches. Never the deprecated `PanResponder`.

## Hard rules

- ❌ Drawer as the **primary** navigation (iOS anti-pattern)
- ❌ Tab bar inside another tab bar
- ❌ More than 5 tabs (split into a "More" section instead)
- ❌ `PanResponder` — use `react-native-gesture-handler`
- ❌ Hard-coded safe-area insets — use `useSafeAreaInsets`
